 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a license\nto sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S8314",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8314-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8314\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a\nlicense to sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill will allow for a restaurant, 2192 Flatbush Avenue, Brooklyn,\nNew York, to obtain a full liquor license. The bill will amend the ABC\n200-foot Law which will authorize the issuing of a retail liquor license\nto a business in the County of Kings, Brooklyn that is within a two-hun-\ndred-foot proximity of a place of worship.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Subdivision 7 of section 64 of the alcoholic beverage control\nlaw is amended by adding a new paragraph (e-17) to read as follows:\n\n(e-17) Notwithstanding the provisions of paragraph(a)of this subdivi-\nsion, the authority may issue a retail license for on-premises consump-\ntion for a premises which shall be located within two hundred feet of a\nbuilding occupied as a church, synagogue or other place of worship,\nprovided such premises constitute a premises for the sale of food or\nbeverages at retail for consumption on the premises located wholly with-\nin the boundaries of the Borough of Brooklyn, county of Kings, bounded\nand described as follows:\n\nALL THAT CERTAIN plot, piece or parcel of land, with the buildings and\nimprovements thereon erect, lying and being in the Borough of Brooklyn,\nCounty of Kings, City and State of New York, bounded and described as\nfollows:\n\nBEGINNING at a point on the westerly side of Flatbush Avenue, distant\n100 feet northerly from the corner formed by the intersection of The\nnortherly side of Avenue R, with the westerly side of Flatbush Avenue,\n\nRUNNING THENCE westerly parallel with Avenue R, 100 feet\n\nTHENCE northerly parallel with Flatbush Avenue, 26 feet, 8 inches to\n\nTHENCE Southerly along the Westerly side of Flatbush Avenue, 26 feet, 8\ninches to the point or place of BEGINNING\n\nTHENCE, Easterly again parallel with Avenue R and part of the distance\nor distant through a party wall, 100 feet to the Westerly side of Flat-\nbush Avenue; SAID PREMISES being known as the Street number 2192-92A\nFlatbush Avenue , Brooklyn NY\n\nBEING THE SAME premises convey to the grantor herein by deed 10/7/97 and\nrecorded in Ree14035, page 1017.\n\nSection 2: states this act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\n\nMany urban communities across the State are facing an uphill battle when\nit comes to maintaining, developing and restoring their historic commu-\nnity areas. Many of these historic areas and districts have existing\ninfrastructures where schools, theatres, universities and churches are\nin close proximity to various store fronts and restaurants. Flatbush,\nthe neighborhood where Lago Kache Restaurant is located, is a destina-\ntion for culture and entertainment. This authentic Haitian establishment\nhas served as the cultural mecca for Haitian cuisine in flatbush Brook-\nlyn. Because of the existing ABC 200-foot law, this establishment has\nbeen restricted in fully serving its customers which makes it a chal-\nlenge to compete, thus impacting the future of business. Furthermore,\nthe religious institution which is within 200-feet, The Redeemed Chris-\ntian Church of God, has expressed that they have no objections to the\nrequest to apply for a full liquor license. An exemption to the ABC\n200-foot Law would allow 2192 Flatbush Avenue to operate with a full\nliquor license they will be able to meet the needs of the growing\nHaitian community and attract more patrons to the new cultural center of\nBrooklyn by offering traditional Haitian cuisines that includ e various\ntypes of liquor as well as cultural alcohol beverages.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nnone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nimmediately\n",
